VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue \u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D. \u000aCASE: BS-08- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  \u000a REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  \u000aResident: Li Chen, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D. \u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS: \u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER INNER, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP \u000aMARKING A CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY SITE: \u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, cribriform type (intermediate nuclear grade), \u000awithout necrosis or calcifications, is present in 6 of 20 blocks ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ) (see note). \u000aDCIS is adjacent to the prior core needle biopsy site. \u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present. An immunoperoxidase study for \u000ae-cadherin confirms the absence of this marker (block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). \u000aThe closest margin to DCIS is the deep margin (0.3cm ). DCIS is not seen \u000aclose to the remaining margins (> 0.3cm ). \u000aCalcifications are associated with columnar cell change with flat atypia. \u000aSome of these areas are adjacent to areas of DCIS. \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies reveal the following for DCIS (blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): \u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE \u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE \u000a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID /neu NEGATIVE (0) \u000aE-cadherin POSITIVE \u000aNote: The patient underwent a core needle biopsy for calcifications that \u000ashowed flat epithelial atypia associated with columnar cell change and \u000acalcifications ( REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). In the excision, there are areas of \u000acribriform DCIS, best seen in block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . Much of the DCIS is present as \u000apagetoid spread in ducts (best seen in block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ). The presence of e- cadherin \u000aexpression in the areas of pagetoid spread support classification of these \u000acells as DCIS. \u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition) Tis NX. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: History of atypia on left core biopsy at BWH REMOVED_DATE  \u000aOperation: Left mammographically directed breast biopsy. \u000aOperative Findings: None given. \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram left breast. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: \u000aA/1. Left breast tissue left upper inner breast short superior, long lateral. \u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip and calcifications in specimen. J. Meyer, MD \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: \u000aThe specimen is received fresh,  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient's name, unit number, \u000aand "#1. Left breast tissue left upper inner breast, short superior-long \u000alateral". The specimen consists of REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2.6cm fragment of fibroadipose \u000atissue, oriented by sutures designating superior (short suture) and lateral \u000a(long suture) margins. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the \u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; \u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange (inked by prosector). A \u000alocalizing wire enters the anterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen is \u000areceived and radiographed demonstrating the localizing wire in place within the \u000aspecimen, adjacent to a gray metallic clip associated with an ill-defined \u000adensity and an adjacent area of faint calcifications. Sectioning demonstrates \u000aa central 1.0cm cavity filed with amorphous material, compatible with a prior \u000acore needle biopsy site, containing the gray metallic surgical clip. There is \u000ascant fibrous tissue in this area and no mass lesions are visible. The cavity \u000ais 0.3cm from the nearest anterior margin, 0.8cm from the nearest posterior \u000amargin, and remote (1.5cm ) from the surrounding margins. The remaining \u000aspecimen is predominantly comprised of soft, lobulated tan/yellow adipose \u000atissue without palpable or visible masses grossly. The entire area of interest \u000ais submitted, sequentially from lateral to medial, along with additional \u000arepresentative fibrous tissue and all closest margins. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Entire area of interest and surrounding tissue, to include closest \u000aanterior and posterior margins, multi frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Closest superior, inferior margins, 2 frags each, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Closest lateral, medial margins, 4 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Fibrous tissue lateral to area of interest, 2 frags, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Fibrous tissue medial to area of interest, 2 frags, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Remainder of breast tissue, multi frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000aDB/ag \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a6/23/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
