VAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Updated Type: Surgical Pathology Pathology Report: MS-08- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE: MS-08- REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken: REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit: Same Day Surgery, WACC 3 Path Subspecialty Service: Breast-1 Results To: MICHELLE C SPECHT, M.D. Resident: Maylee Hsu, M.D. Frozen Section Pathologist: Ben Zion Pilch, M.D. Frozen Section Resident: Thor Stein, M.D. Signed Out by: Melinda J Lerwill, M.D. ********** Addended Report *********** Reason for Addendum #1: Additional Immunohistochemistry Results Reason for Addendum #2: Additional Immunohistochemistry Results CLINICAL DATA: History of DCIS. FINAL DIAGNOSIS: LYMPH NODE (RIGHT AXILLA, SENTINEL # REMOVED_CASE_ID ), EXCISION: THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY IN 1 LYMPH NODE (0/1). NOTE: Keratin-positive tumor cells are not identified on three  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID .3/Cam5.2 immunostained levels. BREAST (RIGHT), EXCISION: 1. DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU WITH TINY FOCUS SUSPICIOUS FOR MICROINVASION, SEE TABLE #1. 2. FOCAL LOBULAR NEOPLASIA. 3. HEALING BIOPSY SITE. NOTE: Prominent cancerization of lobules by the ductal carcinoma in-situ is present. TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS #1 DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU TUMOR SIZE: Involving 10 of 14 blocks. GRADE: 3. CALCIFICATIONS: In the carcinoma in-situ and in benign changes. MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN-SITU: Ductal carcinoma in-situ extends to < 0.1cm from the medial and lateral margins of the specimen in a few foci. STAINS FOR RECEPTORS: Requested. BREAST (LEFT), EXCISION: 1. MULTIPLE SCATTERED SMALL FOCI OF ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA WITH FOCAL CALCIFICATIONS, SEE NOTE. 2. INTRADUCTAL PAPILLOMAS WITH CALCIFICATIONS. 3. FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH CALCIFICATIONS. 4. HEALING BIOPSY SITE. NOTE: Some of the atypical cells demonstrate a pagetoid pattern of spread and cytoplasmic vacuolization, raising consideration of lobular neoplasia. However, these cells are morphologically similar to other cells with more luminal morphologies, and they show membranous positivity for E-cadherin, albeit reduced. Overall, the features seen in the excision favor classification as an atypical ductal hyperplasia rather than an atypical hyperplasia with mixed features, as initially considered on the core biopsy where there was less of the proliferation to evaluate. More conventional ductal atypia is also present. Multiple levels examined. By his/her signature below, the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he/she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses. GROSS DESCRIPTION: Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab,  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME , REMOVE_MRN and "right axillary sentinel node, # REMOVED_CASE_ID ", is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.6cm tan-yellow lymph node, bisected and frozen entirely as FX #1, now submitted as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . Received fresh,  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME , REMOVE_MRN and "left breast", is a 4 x 3 x 1.5cm fibrofatty soft tissue fragment with a localization needle in place and a short stitch marking superior, a long stitch lateral. The long axis is anterior-posterior. On the medial aspect there is a hemorrhagic cavity exposed to the medial surface. Specimen is inked as follows: Medial - green, lateral - yellow, superior - blue, inferior - black. Sectioning reveals a 2 x REMOVED_DATE x 1cm hemorrhagic biopsy cavity that is 1.4cm from the anterior tip, abuts the medial surface, 0.5cm from the yellow inked lateral margin, 0.5cm from the black inked inferior margin, and 0.5cm from the blue inked superior margin, and 0.2cm from the posterior margin. Sections submitted as follows:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Anterior margin,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Posterior margin,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Entire biopsy cavity,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Representative section of parenchyma away from biopsy site. Received fresh,  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME , REMOVE_MRN and "right breast", is a 6 x 4 x 2cm fibrofatty soft tissue fragment with a localization needle in place, a long stitch marking the lateral margin, short superior. Specimen is oriented along the anterior-posterior axis and inked as follows: Superior - blue, inferior - black, medial - green, lateral - yellow. No definite mass or biopsy cavity is identified. The parenchyma shows fibrocystic changes and representative sections submitted as follows:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Anterior tip,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Posterior tip,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Representative sections. INTRA-OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS: FROZEN SECTION # 1; RIGHT AXILLARY SENTINEL NODE # REMOVED_CASE_ID : CARCINOMA IS NOT IDENTIFIED. Final Diagnosis by Melinda J Lerwill M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE: REASON: 1 QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast (Right, block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein. The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows: The tumor cells stain (> 10% of nuclei stained) Some tumor cells stain (1 to <10% of nuclei stained) Rare tumor cells stain (< 1% of nuclei stained) The tumor cells do not stain (0% of nuclei stained) These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital. They have not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary; this laboratory has established and verified the test?s accuracy and precision. Addendum #1 by Atul K Bhan M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE: REASON #2: QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast (Right, block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ): The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein. The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein. The ductal carcinoma in-situ tumor cells show moderate (2+) overexpression of HER-2/c-erbB-2. See note. Note: Definitive microinvasion is not seen on the sections prepared for hormone receptor and HER-2. Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows: The tumor cells stain (> 10% of nuclei stained) Some tumor cells stain (1 to <10% of nuclei stained) Rare tumor cells stain (< 1% of nuclei stained) The tumor cells do not stain (0% of nuclei stained) HER-2/c-erbB-2 quantitation: The tumor cell membrane immunoreactivity is evaluated visually and reported as follows: Do not show overexpression (0 to 1+, no/minimal immunoreactivity or incomplete membrane staining of tumor cells) Show moderate (2+) overexpression (weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in > 10% of tumor cells) Show strong (3+) over overexpression (strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in > 10% of tumor cells) These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital. They have not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary; this laboratory has established and verified the test's accuracy and precision. Addendum #2 by Elena F Brachtel M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE PART A: Lymph Node (Right Axilla, "Sentinel" # REMOVED_CASE_ID ), Excision PART B: Breast (Left), Excision PART C: Breast (Right), Excision PART D: Cryostat Frozen Section (slide only) [report_end] 
